From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:22:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] qt: on Wayland, draw the video on a surface of its own, so
 that hiding a window of the interface does not end the connection

On Wayland the video output drew on the wl_surface of a QWindow that the
video widget held in a window container. Qt 5's Wayland platform destroys a
window's wl_surface whenever it hides the window (QWaylandWindow::setVisible
calls reset()), and Qt hides the container's window whenever the container
is hidden: the page that holds the video is turned or the video widget gets
a new parent (Ctrl+L, the playlist, moves the playing video into the
playlist's panel and back), the main window is made again (its flags
changed). The video output thread went on drawing: Mesa attached its next
picture to the destroyed surface, or the video output resized its viewport,
and the compositor ended the connection ("wl_display#1: error 0: invalid
object N" or "wp_viewport#N: error 3: the wl_surface for this viewport no
longer exists"), so VLC closed. In the rig 15 of 15 runs with Ctrl+L during
playback ended so, 4 to 41 ms after Qt destroyed the surface, at 100, 125 and
150 %, on 2D and 3D outputs, with 2D and 3D films, on two GPUs.

As VLC 4's Wayland compositor does (modules/gui/qt/maininterface/
compositor_wayland_module.c), the video surface is now the video output's
own: the interface creates it with a wl_compositor and a wl_subcompositor
of its own, on an event queue of its own, with an empty input region, and
gives it to the video output in place of a Qt window's. A plain widget holds
its place in the layout and takes the mouse and the keys, as on X11 (the
native window's cursor mirroring and event forwarding of 3d-0046 go with
the window). While that widget is shown, the video surface is a
desynchronized sub-surface of the main window's surface, at the widget's
place (plus the frame of a client-side decoration, as Qt places its own
sub-surfaces), with the buffer scale Qt gives its own surfaces; the role is
destroyed when the widget is hidden and made again when it is shown or when
the main window, shown again, has a new surface. The surface itself is
destroyed only when the video output has released the window, so the video
output never names a destroyed object. The stereo declaration and the
viewport stay the video output's, on the same surface.

VLC 3.0.x upstream removed its Qt Wayland video window (04d8fcb9, "qt: remove
dysfunctional Wayland window"); VLC 4 already draws on a surface of its own,
and its 84e7e017 ("qt: do not hide window on close on Wayland") records the
same behaviour of Qt before 6.9. The change is for VLC Stereo only.

Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-11-15
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
---
